---
// Plain-language privacy policy. Every statement here describes what the
// code actually does; if you change data handling, update this page.
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../content/site';

const app = siteConfig.appName;
const email = siteConfig.contactEmail;
---
<LegalLayout title="Privacy policy">
  <p class="lead">
    {app} is a small, invite-only app for two people who coach each other at the gym. This page explains, in plain
    language, what data the app keeps, who can see it, and how to delete it.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Only you and your paired partner can see your plans, logs, photos and messages.</li>
    <li>We don't show ads, run analytics, or sell or share your data with anyone.</li>
    <li>You can delete your account and everything in it yourself, any time, from Settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="google">What we get from Google</h2>
  <p>
    You sign in with Google. We ask Google only for the basic sign-in scopes (<code>openid</code>, <code>email</code>
    and <code>profile</code>) and keep:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your Google account ID</strong>: a number that identifies your Google account. This is how we recognise you.</li>
    <li><strong>Your email address</strong>: shown to super users (see below) and used to decide whether you can sign up.</li>
    <li><strong>Your name</strong>: used as your starting display name. You can change your display name in Settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Your profile picture's web address</strong>: shown as your avatar.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your name, profile picture and email are refreshed from Google each time you sign in. We don't request or receive
    any other Google data: no contacts, calendar, Drive, Gmail or anything else. We never see your Google password.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yours">What you add</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Profile and goals</strong>: height, start and target weight, goal, target date, units and time zone.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight log</strong>: your weigh-ins by date.</li>
    <li><strong>Plans</strong>: your workout and diet plan (usually written by your partner), including exercise photos and links.</li>
    <li><strong>Daily logs</strong>: exercises done with sets, reps and weight; meals and calories; water; calories burned.</li>
    <li><strong>Photos</strong>: gym photos, chat photos and exercise photos.</li>
    <li><strong>Chat and social</strong>: messages, reactions, nudges and notes of the day.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The app also derives streaks, milestones and an activity feed from what you log.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Photos are cleaned on your device before upload.</strong> They're resized and re-encoded in your browser,
    which removes location (GPS) and camera details, and the server rejects any photo that still contains them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who can see your data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You</strong>, of course.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your paired partner</strong>, while you're paired. They can see your profile, weights, plan, daily logs,
      gym photos, exercise photos and activity, and you share one chat. Your partner can also edit your plan (and if you
      turn on self-editing, so can you). Nobody else can be paired with you; pairing is strictly one-to-one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Super users</strong> are the people who run this {app} and send invites. In the app they can see every
      account's email, display name, sign-up date and whether it's active; the list of invites with each invite's note
      and who used it; and how much photo storage each person uses (totals only). They can deactivate and reactivate
      accounts. <strong>The app gives super users no way to read your plans, logs, weights, photos or messages.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The operator's hosting account.</strong> As with any app, whoever controls the Cloudflare account that hosts
      {app} could technically access the database and photo storage directly, outside the app. We don't do this except
      when needed to keep the service running or when legally required.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="storage">Where it's stored</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The app runs on <strong>Cloudflare Workers</strong>.</li>
    <li>Your account and everything you log are stored in a <strong>Cloudflare D1</strong> database.</li>
    <li>
      Photos are stored in a private <strong>Cloudflare R2</strong> bucket. They have no public web address: they're
      only sent to a signed-in user allowed to see them (you or your partner).
    </li>
    <li>
      Cloudflare keeps short-lived technical logs of requests (such as the page or API address, time and any errors)
      to help keep the service running. These expire automatically after a few days.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cloudflare's database keeps a rolling backup history for disaster recovery. Deleted data drops out of that history
      on its own within about a month.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and your device</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>spotter_session</code></strong> keeps you signed in. It's a random token (we store only a scrambled
      version of it). It's sent only to this site and isn't readable by scripts. It expires 30 days after you last open
      the app (each day you use the app pushes that back). Signing out ends it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>spotter_oauth</code></strong> exists only while you're signing in with Google (at most 10 minutes).
      It protects the sign-in from tampering and, if you came from an invite link, carries the invite.
    </li>
    <li>Your light/dark theme choice is saved in your browser only.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    To slow down abuse, the server counts sign-in, pairing and test-notification attempts per
    <strong>IP address</strong>. These counters are short-lived: any counter over a day old is deleted the next time
    someone signs in, enters a pairing code or sends a test notification.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notifications">Notifications</h2>
  <p>
    Notifications are off until you turn them on with a button; the app never asks on its own. When you turn them on,
    your browser creates a <strong>push subscription</strong> for that device, and we store:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the subscription's address at your browser's push service and the keys used to encrypt messages to it,</li>
    <li>your browser's name and version (its "user agent"), so you can tell devices apart,</li>
    <li>when the device was added and when a notification last reached it,</li>
    <li>your notification settings (which kinds you want, and whether to hide message previews).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Notifications are delivered through your browser maker's push service: <strong>Google</strong> (Chrome, Edge and
    Android), <strong>Apple</strong> (Safari and iPhone/iPad) or <strong>Mozilla</strong> (Firefox). The content is
    end-to-end encrypted to your device, so the push service can't read it, but it does see that a notification was
    sent, when, and roughly how big it was.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notifications only come from your partner's activity (messages, nudges, notes, gym photos, plan changes, milestones
    and finished workouts), plus test notifications you send yourself. Because they can appear on a lock screen, they
    <strong>never include weights, body measurements or calories</strong>. Message notifications show the start of the
    message unless you turn on "hide message previews".
  </p>
  <p>
    A device's subscription is deleted when you sign out on it, when you turn notifications off there, when its push
    service reports it no longer exists, or when you delete your account. A device also stops getting notifications once
    its sign-in expires (30 days after you last opened the app there); its subscription is then deleted the next time a
    notification would have been sent to you. You can turn individual
    kinds of notification off in Settings at any time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="third-parties">Other services your device talks to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Google</strong>, when you sign in, and to load your profile picture from Google's image servers.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>YouTube</strong>, only for video links in a plan. When you open an exercise's photos and videos, the video's
      thumbnail loads from YouTube's image server; the video player (YouTube's privacy-enhanced
      <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code> version) loads only when you tap play.
    </li>
    <li>Other links in a plan open the linked website in a new tab.</li>
    <li>Your browser's push service, if you turn notifications on (see above).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There are no ads, no analytics or tracking scripts, and no social media widgets. Fonts are served from {app} itself.
    <strong>We don't sell or share your data.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2 id="unpairing">When you unpair</h2>
  <p>Either partner can unpair at any time in Settings. When you do:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>You immediately stop seeing each other's data.</li>
    <li>Your shared chat (messages, and the reactions, nudges and notes of the day between you) is deleted.</li>
    <li>
      Each of you keeps your own profile, weights, plan, logs and gym photos. A plan your partner wrote for you stays with
      you, and you can then edit it yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      Photos you sent in the chat stay stored in your own account, where the app no longer shows them to anyone. They're
      deleted with your account.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deletion">Deleting your account</h2>
  <p>
    Go to <strong>Settings → Delete account</strong>. After you confirm twice, the deletion happens immediately and
    can't be undone:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your account, profile, weights, plan, daily logs, streaks, milestones and activity are deleted.</li>
    <li>Your gym photos, chat photos and the photos on your plan are deleted from storage.</li>
    <li>Your notification settings and every device's push subscription are deleted.</li>
    <li>You're unpaired; your shared chat, reactions, nudges and notes are deleted.</li>
    <li>You're signed out everywhere.</li>
    <li>If you're a super user, invites you created are deleted (people who already joined keep their accounts).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your former partner keeps their own data. That includes the plan you wrote for them and any exercise photos you
    added to it, since those are part of their plan; their plan simply no longer says who set it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Can't sign in? Email <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a> from the address on your account and we'll delete it
    for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If we change how your data is handled, we'll update this page and its effective date. For significant changes we'll
    let you know in the app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions or requests about your data: <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.</p>
</LegalLayout>
